From: Hoang Viet Pham <hoang.pham@adtran.com>
Date: Mon, 15 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] airoha: fix flood-packet FDB lookup ignoring VLAN context

airoha_check_flood_packet() is the last gate in airoha_ppe_tx_handler()
before airoha_ppe_general_bind() actually binds a flow to the hardware
PPE FOE entry: it looks up the packet's destination MAC in the bridge
FDB and only allows the bind when the FDB shows that MAC as reachable
through the same device the packet is about to egress on.

Both call sites passed a hardcoded vid=0 to this helper's `vid`
parameter. The function has two internal FDB lookup paths and both
ignored real VLAN context in favor of that constant:

1. Main path: once the egress device is confirmed to be a bridge port,
   it looked up br_fdb_find_rcu(br, addr, vid) with vid still 0. On
   this target br0 is VLAN-aware and every port (lan1-4, wifi5g,
   w5gM, ...) carries a single PVID-untagged VLAN (4094), so real FDB
   entries are keyed by vlan 4094, not vlan 0. The lookup with vid=0
   therefore always missed, regardless of the destination MAC or
   device, and the function fell through to `return 0` (treat as
   flood) every time.

2. VLAN fallback path: when the egress device isn't a bridge port but
   the packet itself carries an 802.1Q tag with no matching VLAN
   sub-device, the code scans every bridge master in the system for
   an FDB entry -- again with the same hardcoded vid=0, instead of
   the VID actually carried in the packet's own VLAN tag.

Both silently blocked hardware PPE binding for any flow whose egress
faces a bridged client -- LAN or WiFi -- while leaving already-working
paths (e.g. upstream traffic egressing fport==2/WAN, which returns
earlier via airoha_tunnel_hook_tx) unaffected. That asymmetry is what
made "wifi to wan has no bind entries" look wifi-specific: the same
gate also silently dropped downstream-to-LAN-client binds, but nobody
had traffic profiling to notice on the wired side.

The right VID differs per path and is only known once the function has
already done its own device resolution (DSA slave lookup, bridge-port
check, VLAN-tag-vs-subdevice disambiguation), so pushing this to the
callers would mean duplicating that resolution logic there instead of
fixing it once. Drop the now-dead `vid` parameter entirely and resolve
the correct VID internally at each lookup site instead: the egress
device's PVID via br_vlan_get_pvid_rcu() for the main (untagged) path,
and the VID out of the packet's own VLAN tag for the fallback
(explicitly tagged) path.

Verified on SmartRG SDG-8716v: bridge fdb show reported both a wired
client (dev lan2, vlan 4094) and a wifi client (dev wifi5g, vlan 4094,
offload) with real conntrack traffic, yet
/sys/kernel/debug/airoha-eth/ppe/bind stayed empty for both until the
main-path fix; airoha_ppe_general_bind() now runs and hardware-binds
flows in both directions. The VLAN fallback path was not exercised by
that traffic (untagged PVID 4094) but shares the same hardcoded-vid
root cause and is fixed on the same basis.

Signed-off-by: Hoang Viet Pham <hoang.pham@adtran.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/arht_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/arht_ppe.c
@@ -332,7 +332,7 @@ static struct net_device* arht_get_vland
 	return dev;
 }
 
-static int airoha_check_flood_packet(struct sk_buff *skb, u16 vid, u32 tag)
+static int airoha_check_flood_packet(struct sk_buff *skb, u32 tag)
 {
 	struct net_bridge *br;
 	struct net_device *dev = NULL, *tmp_dev = NULL;
@@ -369,7 +369,14 @@ static int airoha_check_flood_packet(str
 			tmp_dev = arht_get_vlandev(skb);
 			if(!tmp_dev){
 				struct net_device *br_dev;
+				u16 tag_vid;
+
 				addr = skb->data;
+				/* The packet itself carries an 802.1Q tag but no
+				 * matching VLAN sub-device was found; the FDB
+				 * entries were learned under that tag's VID.
+				 */
+				tag_vid = ntohs(vlan_eth_hdr(skb)->h_vlan_TCI) & VLAN_VID_MASK;
 				rcu_read_lock();
 				for_each_netdev_rcu(dev_net(skb->dev), br_dev) {
 					if (!netif_is_bridge_master(br_dev))
@@ -377,7 +384,7 @@ static int airoha_check_flood_packet(str
 					struct net_bridge *br_tmp = netdev_priv(br_dev);
 					if (!br_tmp)
 						continue;
-					struct net_bridge_fdb_entry *f_tmp = br_fdb_find_rcu(br_tmp, addr, vid);
+					struct net_bridge_fdb_entry *f_tmp = br_fdb_find_rcu(br_tmp, addr, tag_vid);
 					if (f_tmp && f_tmp->dst) {
 						rcu_read_unlock();
 						return 1;
@@ -404,12 +411,21 @@ static int airoha_check_flood_packet(str
 	}
 
 	addr = skb->data;
-	
+
 	rcu_read_lock();
-	f = br_fdb_find_rcu(br, addr, vid);
+	/* FDB entries are keyed by whatever VID tmp_dev's PVID resolves to
+	 * (0 on a non-VLAN-filtering bridge, the real PVID otherwise) --
+	 * never a caller-supplied constant.
+	 */
+	{
+		u16 pvid = 0;
+
+		br_vlan_get_pvid_rcu(tmp_dev, &pvid);
+		f = br_fdb_find_rcu(br, addr, pvid);
+	}
 	if (f && f->dst){
 		dev = f->dst->dev;
-	}	
+	}
 	rcu_read_unlock();
 
 	if (dev && dev == tmp_dev){
@@ -1307,7 +1323,7 @@ static void airoha_ppe_pon_sfu_bind(stru
 {
 	if (!airoha_is_pon_point_to_point_mode(skb)) {
 		//if not point to ponit mode, need to check mac table in kernel
-	    if (!airoha_check_flood_packet(skb, 0, pinfo->stag)) {
+	    if (!airoha_check_flood_packet(skb, pinfo->stag)) {
 	        return;
 	    }
 	}
@@ -1608,7 +1624,7 @@ int airoha_ppe_tx_handler
 	}
 
 	//not flood packet, for bridge wan mode
-	if (airoha_check_flood_packet(skb, 0, pinfo->stag)) {
+	if (airoha_check_flood_packet(skb, pinfo->stag)) {
         airoha_ppe_general_bind(glb_eth->ppe, hwe, skb, pinfo, fport);
 		spin_unlock_bh(&ppe_lock);
 		return 1;
